WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.731
Batch size tuning for inference is the practice of choosing how much work the runtime should process together when it serves prompts and generates tokens.

2
00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:17.440
It sounds simple, but it sits at the intersection of model size, context length, memory, hardware, and user expectations.

3
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:29.491
In practical terms, batch settings decide whether the system handles one request at a time, groups pieces of work together, or keeps multiple active requests moving through the model at once.

4
00:00:29.491 --> 00:00:32.261
This is different from training batch size.

5
00:00:32.261 --> 00:00:38.324
During training, a batch is tied to examples, loss calculation, gradients, and weight updates.

6
00:00:38.324 --> 00:00:41.842
During inference, the model weights are already fixed.

7
00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:50.674
The question becomes operational: how can the runtime use available compute efficiently while still returning useful responses quickly?

8
00:00:50.674 --> 00:00:58.159
A setting that makes sense for training documentation may not translate directly to a chat server or a local assistant.

9
00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:02.426
It also helps to separate prompt processing from token generation.

10
00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:09.387
When a prompt is submitted, the runtime has to process the existing context before the first new token appears.

11
00:01:09.387 --> 00:01:12.755
After that, generation usually happens step by step.

12
00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:22.561
Batch settings can affect these phases differently, so a configuration that improves prompt ingestion may not produce the smoothest streaming response.

13
00:01:22.561 --> 00:01:25.181
The right answer depends on the workload.

14
00:01:25.181 --> 00:01:31.019
A single-user local coding assistant usually values responsiveness and stable memory use.

15
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:37.007
A multi-user API service may accept slightly more latency if it can increase total throughput.

16
00:01:37.007 --> 00:01:49.208
There is no universal best batch size because the best operating point changes with model architecture, quantization, runtime, hardware, prompt length, concurrency, and reliability goals.

17
00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:56.992
A practical way to think about this is to start with the user experience you want, then work backward to the runtime settings.

18
00:01:56.992 --> 00:02:03.205
If the system is a private assistant, the tuning target may be quick feedback and steady streaming.

19
00:02:03.205 --> 00:02:08.070
If it is an internal API, the target may be more completed requests per minute.

20
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.861
Batch size is only meaningful when it is tied to one of those operating goals.
